{
  "evidence": "TEXT_F: 20,000 nurses/8,000 GPs/3,000 midwives; integrate physical–mental–social care with parity for mental health; end 15‑minute care visits and strengthen child protection; recruit 1,000 extra border staff with full exit checks; stronger terror controls and protect neighbourhood policing; maintain a minimum, credible independent nuclear deterrent and strengthen the Armed Forces Covenant; cut the deficit every year with no new borrowing‑funded spending, cap structural welfare, restrict Winter Fuel Payments to the richest 5%, two‑year wait for in‑work benefits for new EU arrivals; promote the Living Wage, gender pay‑gap reporting, race‑equality strategy, and advance LGBT rights. TEXT_C: Increase UK health spending, protect the NHS from privatisation, expand mental‑health funding, integrate health and social care; expand free childcare to 30 hours and raise Carer’s Allowance; abolish the Bedroom Tax, uprate benefits with cost of living, raise work allowance by 20%; oppose blanket data retention and renew targeted, overseen counter‑extremism; oppose renewal of the UK nuclear deterrent; support EU membership with a double‑majority requirement; fracking moratorium and strong renewables push.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts strongly satisfy the electorate’s top Care/Harm priority, but TEXT_F adds clearer alignment with Loyalty/Authority via stronger defence, border control and policing, and with Proportionality through fiscal restraint, capped welfare and targeted benefits. TEXT_C leans more toward equality‑oriented welfare expansion and institutional change while being weaker on loyalty/authority signals (e.g., opposing nuclear renewal), which are relatively more important than equality and sanctity in this profile.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
